    subjectLabel: "Science",
    topic: "Our Environment",
    front: "Define Biological Magnification (Biomagnification) with an example.",
    back: "Biological Magnification is the phenomenon whereby the concentration of non-biodegradable harmful chemical pollutants (such as DDT, pesticides, heavy metals) increases progressively at each successive trophic level in a food chain.\nReason: These chemicals cannot be metabolised or excreted by organisms and accumulate in body fats.\nExample: Phytoplankton (0.04 ppm DDT) → Small fish (0.5 ppm) → Large fish (2 ppm) → Fish-eating birds/Humans (25 ppm - maximum concentration).",
    keywords: ["Non-biodegradable chemicals", "Increases at each successive trophic level", "Maximum at top trophic level (humans/birds)"],
    chapterLink: "/resources/our-environment/"
  },
